Quadlets: podman containers as systemd services
===============================================

A file here named NAME.container (or .pod, .volume, .network, .kube) becomes the
system service NAME.service at the next `systemctl daemon-reload` or boot. There
are two places to put one, and which you pick decides who runs it:

  System lane: this directory, /etc/containers/systemd/   (as root)
    Runs rootful, starts at boot with nobody logged in, needs no linger.
    [Install]  WantedBy=multi-user.target
    The cleanest fit for a server's services (US-SRV-9).

  User lane: ~/.config/containers/systemd/                (as you)
    Runs rootless, as your user. Starts at boot only with linger on:
      loginctl enable-linger "$USER"        (once; PengyOS lets you do your own)
    [Install]  WantedBy=default.target

Example, /etc/containers/systemd/web.container:

  [Unit]
  Description=nginx
  [Container]
  Image=docker.io/library/nginx:stable
  PublishPort=8080:80
  Environment=TZ=America/Toronto      # Environment=, not Env= (an unknown key drops the unit)
  Volume=/data/web:/usr/share/nginx/html:ro,Z
  [Service]
  Restart=always
  [Install]
  WantedBy=multi-user.target

  systemctl daemon-reload && systemctl start web
  pengyos-firewall allow 8080/tcp --from 10.0.23.0/24    # the firewall is default-deny

Output: a container's stdout and stderr go to the journal, and stay there after
the container is gone:
  journalctl -u web               (system lane)
  journalctl --user -u web        (user lane)
`podman logs` does not work for quadlet containers (container.d/10-pengyos-log.conf
sets LogDriver=passthrough; that file says how to choose another driver).

Two traps:
  * A file with a key quadlet does not know leaves NO unit: `systemctl start web`
    says "Unit web.service not found". The reason is only in the generator's log:
      sudo /usr/lib/systemd/system-generators/podman-system-generator --dryrun   (system)
      /usr/lib/systemd/user-generators/podman-user-generator --user --dryrun (user)
    or journalctl -b | grep quadlet
  * `systemctl is-enabled web` answers "generated", never "enabled". That is how
    systemd labels any generated unit. With an [Install] section the service
    starts at boot (the generator wires it into the target itself).
